@@ -0,0 +1,177 @@
+# HwaFlow patch — install guide
+
+This folder is a **drop-in patch** for your `HwaFlow-main` repo. It adds four
+API routes, one server-side client, and one optional UI panel. It does **not**
+change any existing table, RLS policy, page, or component behaviour, so your
+current OCR → translate → proofread pipeline keeps working exactly as before.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Copy the files
+
+From the root of your HwaFlow repo:
+
+```bash
+cp -r xian-scan-bridge/hwaflow-patch/lib/engine.ts                 lib/engine.ts
+cp -r xian-scan-bridge/hwaflow-patch/app/api/engine                app/api/engine
+cp -r xian-scan-bridge/hwaflow-patch/components/chapters/engine-panel.tsx \
+      components/chapters/engine-panel.tsx
+cp    xian-scan-bridge/hwaflow-patch/supabase/migrations/20261026130000_engine_purge.sql \
+      supabase/migrations/20261026130000_engine_purge.sql
+```
+
+Result:
+
+```
+lib/engine.ts
+app/api/engine/jobs/route.ts
+app/api/engine/jobs/[id]/route.ts
+app/api/engine/jobs/[id]/approve/route.ts
+app/api/engine/jobs/[id]/reject/route.ts
+app/api/engine/jobs/[id]/[kind]/[page]/route.ts
+components/chapters/engine-panel.tsx
+supabase/migrations/20261026130000_engine_purge.sql   (optional)
+```
+
+No new npm packages. `server-only` and `@supabase/ssr` are already dependencies.
+Run `supabase db push` once to apply the optional migration.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Add the environment variables
+
+`.env.local` (and the same in Cloudflare → Workers & Pages → your HwaFlow
+project → Settings → Environment variables, marked **Secret/Encrypted**):
+
+```dotenv
+ENGINE_API_URL=https://engine.hwaflow.space
+ENGINE_WEB_TOKEN=<the SAME random string as the Worker's WEB_TOKEN secret>
+ENGINE_PURGE_SUPABASE_ORIGINALS=1   # optional; see below
+```
+
+`ENGINE_WEB_TOKEN` is **never** exposed to the browser — no `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
+prefix, ever. It's only read inside `lib/engine.ts`, which is `server-only`.
+
+`ENGINE_PURGE_SUPABASE_ORIGINALS` is **off by default**. Turn it on only after
+applying the optional migration. It makes the engine's Approve button also
+delete the user's original WebPs from the Supabase `pages` bucket and mark
+those `chapter_pages` rows `purged` — implementing the `purge_on_approval`
+policy your docs already promise. Leave it off if you want to keep originals in
+Supabase (the R2 raw copies are always deleted regardless).
+
+All three names are already appended (blank) to `.env.example` in
+`HwaFlow-with-engine.zip`.
+
+---
+
+## 2b. Automatic Kaggle start (the "kernel push" mode)
+
+This patch does **not** need to change for auto-start. Two additions are
+already in place:
+
+- `app/api/engine/jobs/route.ts` reads `text_blocks.final_text` and sends it as
+  `translations[page][block]` in the job metadata. **This is only an offline
+  fallback.** The primary translator is XianScan's own
+  `GeminiTranslationProvider` running inside the Kaggle kernel
+  (`KAGGLE_TRANSLATION_MODE=gemini`). The user rejected the older HwaFlow
+  Gemini output as unreliable, so it is never used when the kernel has
+  internet + a Gemini key.
+- `lib/engine.ts` forwards it and exposes `EngineJob.kaggle` — a small status
+  object (`queued → uploading → dataset_pending → running → complete`) so the
+  existing poll loop can show what Kaggle is doing.
+
+Everything else is in `xian-scan-bridge/kaggle/README.md`. If the Worker has
+no `KAGGLE_USERNAME`/`KAGGLE_KEY`, it behaves exactly like the older pull
+bridge and `EngineJob.kaggle` is `null` — no code path breaks.
+
+> **`engine.hwaflow.space` is a proposed subdomain.** Replace it with your
+> real Worker hostname if Cloudflare gave you a `*.workers.dev` URL instead.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Mount the panel (optional but recommended)
+
+In `components/chapters/workspace-view.tsx`:
+
+```tsx
+// near the other ./components/chapters imports
+import { EnginePanel } from "./engine-panel";
+```
+
+Then insert it just **after** the closing `</div>` of `<div className="ch-header">`
+and immediately before `{uploadErrors.length > 0 ? (`:
+
+```tsx
+              </div>   {/* end ch-header */}
+
+              <EnginePanel chapterId={chapter.id} canApprove={canManage} />
+
+              {uploadErrors.length > 0 ? (
+```
+
+Use `canManage` (not `canApprove`) — `canApprove` in this file is narrower
+(`canManage && status === "submitted"`) and would hide the image Approve button
+while a chapter is still `ready`. `onApproved` is optional; leave it out if you
+want image approval and chapter approval to stay as two separate clicks.
+
+> If you don't want to touch the workspace yet, you can test the whole backend
+> with curl (step 5) and mount the panel afterwards.
+
+---
+
+## 4. How it plugs into what you already have
+
+| Already in HwaFlow | What the patch does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `chapter_pages.original_path` → Supabase Storage `pages` bucket | `POST /api/engine/jobs` reads those originals and forwards them to the bridge |
+| `series.source_language` (`ko`/`ja`/`zh`) | Mapped to engine OCR codes (`ko`/`ja`/`zh-Hans`) |
+| `glossary_terms` (active terms, per series) | Forwarded into the CLI's `--glossary` so names stay consistent |
+| `requirePipelineViewer()` session + rate-limit gate | Reused for every new engine route |
+| `storage_state` + `groups.retention_policy` (documented, unimplemented) | Optional migration + `ENGINE_PURGE_SUPABASE_ORIGINALS=1` finally implements purge-on-approval in Supabase too |
+
+Nothing here writes to your `text_blocks` table. If you later want the engine's
+`regions.json` (OCR boxes + source/target text) to appear in your bubble
+editor, read `GET /api/engine/jobs/:id/regions/:page` and feed it into
+`setPanelBlocksAction` — the shapes are already compatible (`bbox`,
+`sourceText`/`textSource`, `textTarget`). That is a phase-2, ~30-line change.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Test without touching the UI
+
+```bash
+# start the site
+npm run dev
+
+# create a job for a chapter that already has uploaded panels
+curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/engine/jobs \
+  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
+  -H "cookie: <your session cookie>" \
+  -d '{"chapterId":"<a real chapter uuid>"}'
+# -> {"jobId":"...","status":"pending","pageCount":N}
+
+curl -H "cookie: <your session cookie>" \
+  http://localhost:3000/api/engine/jobs/<jobId>
+# -> {"status":"done","urls":[...]} once the bridge finishes
+
+curl -X POST -H "cookie: <your session cookie>" \
+  http://localhost:3000/api/engine/jobs/<jobId>/approve
+# -> {"status":"approved"} and the raw images are gone
+```
+
+---
+
+## 6. Role check (recommended)
+
+`approve/route.ts` currently uses the same signed-in + rate-limited gate as the
+rest of the pipeline routes. If only Owners/Managers should approve the final
+images, add your existing `canManage`-style check there:
+
+```ts
+// after `const auth = await requirePipelineViewer();`
+// const allowed = await canManageChapter(auth.viewer, chapterId);
+// if (!allowed) return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
+```
+
+`workspace-view.tsx` already hides the button for non-managers via
+`canApprove`; the server check closes the direct-URL hole.
diff --git a/lib/engine.ts b/lib/engine.ts
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..37f8aa0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/engine.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+/**
+ * Server-side client for the XianScan Engine Bridge Worker.
+ *
+ * The browser NEVER sees ENGINE_WEB_TOKEN and NEVER talks to the engine
+ * directly. All traffic goes website -> Worker, and the engine itself only
+ * makes outbound requests to the Worker (it never accepts inbound traffic).
+ */
+
+import "server-only";
+
+function config() {
+  const apiUrl = (process.env.ENGINE_API_URL || "").replace(/\/$/, "");
+  const token = process.env.ENGINE_WEB_TOKEN || "";
+  if (!apiUrl || !token) {
+    throw new Error(
+      "Image engine is not configured. Set ENGINE_API_URL and ENGINE_WEB_TOKEN in .env.local / Cloudflare secrets."
+    );
+  }
+  return { apiUrl, token };
+}
+
+export type EnginePage = {
+  index: number;
+  status: string;
+  mimeType?: string;
+  error?: string;
+};
+
+export type EngineJob = {
+  jobId: string;
+  chapterId: string;
+  status: "pending" | "working" | "done" | "failed" | "approved" | "rejected";
+  sourceLang: string;
+  targetLang: string;
+  pageCount: number;
+  pages: EnginePage[];
+  createdAt: string;
+  claimedAt: string | null;
+  finishedAt: string | null;
+  approvedAt: string | null;
+  error: string | null;
+  // Present when the Worker is driving an auto-started Kaggle kernel.
+  kaggle?: {
+    mode: string;
+    status: string;
+    ref: string | null;
+    message: string | null;
+    version?: number | null;
+    pushedAt?: string | null;
+    lastError?: string | null;
+  } | null;
+  urls: Array<{ index: number; raw: string; final: string; approved: string; regions: string }>;
+};
+
+async function engineFetch(path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<Response> {
+  const { apiUrl, token } = config();
+  const response = await fetch(`${apiUrl}${path}`, {
+    ...init,
+    headers: {
+      authorization: `Bearer ${token}`,
+      ...(init.headers || {}),
+    },
+    cache: "no-store",
+  });
+  return response;
+}
+
+async function engineJson<T>(path: string, init?: RequestInit): Promise<T> {
+  const response = await engineFetch(path, init);
+  const text = await response.text();
+  if (!response.ok) {
+    let message = text;
+    try {
+      const parsed = JSON.parse(text) as { message?: string; error?: string };
+      message = parsed.message || parsed.error || text;
+    } catch { /* keep raw text */ }
+    throw new Error(`Engine request failed (${response.status}): ${message.slice(0, 300)}`);
+  }
+  return JSON.parse(text) as T;
+}
+
+export type CreateEngineJobInput = {
+  chapterId: string;
+  pages: Array<{ blob: Blob; mimeType: string }>;
+  sourceLang: string;
+  targetLang?: string;
+  glossary?: Array<{ sourceTerm: string; targetTerm: string; category: string }>;
+  /** translations[enginePageIndex][blockIndex] = approved English text. */
+  translations?: string[][];
+};
+
+/** POST /v1/jobs — one multipart upload containing every page of the chapter. */
+export async function createEngineJob(
+  input: CreateEngineJobInput
+): Promise<{ jobId: string; status: string; pageCount: number }> {
+  if (input.pages.length === 0) throw new Error("This chapter has no uploaded pages.");
+  if (input.pages.length > 40) throw new Error("This bridge handles at most 40 pages per chapter.");
+
+  const form = new FormData();
+  form.set(
+    "meta",
+    JSON.stringify({
+      chapterId: input.chapterId,
+      pageCount: input.pages.length,
+      sourceLang: input.sourceLang,
+      targetLang: input.targetLang || "en",
+      glossary: input.glossary || [],
+      translations: input.translations || [],
+    })
+  );
+  input.pages.forEach((page, index) => {
+    const extension = page.mimeType === "image/webp" ? "webp" : "png";
+    form.set(`page-${index}`, page.blob, `${String(index + 1).padStart(3, "0")}.${extension}`);
+  });
+
+  return engineJson("/v1/jobs", { method: "POST", body: form });
+}
+
+export async function getEngineJob(jobId: string): Promise<EngineJob> {
+  return engineJson(`/v1/jobs/${encodeURIComponent(jobId)}`);
+}
+
+/** Approve = keep the final PNGs for 7 days, delete the user's raw uploads now. */
+export async function approveEngineJob(jobId: string): Promise<EngineJob> {
+  return engineJson(`/v1/jobs/${encodeURIComponent(jobId)}/approve`, {
+    method: "POST",
+    headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
+    body: "{}",
+  });
+}
+
+export async function rejectEngineJob(jobId: string): Promise<EngineJob> {
+  return engineJson(`/v1/jobs/${encodeURIComponent(jobId)}/reject`, {
+    method: "POST",
+    headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
+    body: "{}",
+  });
+}
+
+/** Streams an image/JSON blob from the Worker so no token ever reaches the browser. */
+export async function proxyEngineAsset(
+  jobId: string,
+  kind: "raw" | "final" | "approved" | "clean" | "regions",
+  page: number
+): Promise<Response> {
+  const response = await engineFetch(
+    `/v1/jobs/${encodeURIComponent(jobId)}/${kind}/${page}`
+  );
+  if (!response.ok) {
+    return Response.json(
+      { error: "engine_asset_unavailable", message: `That image is no longer available (${response.status}).` },
+      { status: response.status === 404 ? 404 : 502 }
+    );
+  }
+  const headers = new Headers();
+  const contentType = response.headers.get("content-type");
+  headers.set("content-type", contentType || (kind === "regions" ? "application/json" : "image/png"));
+  headers.set("cache-control", "private, max-age=300");
+  return new Response(await response.arrayBuffer(), { status: 200, headers });
+}
diff --git a/supabase/migrations/20261105000000_engine_purge.sql b/supabase/migrations/20261105000000_engine_purge.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b5d6f8b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/supabase/migrations/20261105000000_engine_purge.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+-- OPTIONAL but recommended: implements the "purge originals on approval" policy
+-- that docs/ARCHITECTURE.md already promises and groups.retention_policy already
+-- stores, but that no code in the original repo actually performed.
+--
+-- Run with the rest of your migrations (`supabase db push`), then enable it in
+-- HwaFlow with ENGINE_PURGE_SUPABASE_ORIGINALS=1. Without that env var this
+-- function is never called, so applying this migration is safe.
+--
+-- It only flips metadata. The actual Storage objects are removed by
+-- app/api/engine/jobs/[id]/approve/route.ts using the caller's own client, so
+-- the existing "pages_delete" RLS policy still governs who can delete.
+
+set search_path = public, extensions;
+
+create or replace function public.purge_chapter_originals(p_chapter uuid)
+returns int
+language plpgsql
+security definer
+set search_path = ''
+as $$
+declare
+  v_count int := 0;
+begin
+  -- Managers/Owners only. can_manage_chapter() is already defined by the
+  -- chapters migration and checks the caller's group role.
+  if not public.can_manage_chapter(p_chapter) then
+    raise exception 'Only a manager can purge chapter originals.'
+      using errcode = 'HF001';
+  end if;
+
+  -- Respect the group's swappable retention policy. 'keep' = do nothing.
+  if exists (
+    select 1
+    from public.chapters c
+    join public.series s on s.id = c.series_id
+    join public.groups g on g.id = s.group_id
+    where c.id = p_chapter
+      and coalesce(g.retention_policy, 'purge_on_approval') = 'keep'
+  ) then
+    return 0;
+  end if;
+
+  update public.chapter_pages
+     set storage_state = 'purged'
+   where chapter_id = p_chapter
+     and storage_state = 'stored';
+
+  get diagnostics v_count = row_count;
+  return v_count;
+end;
+$$;
+
+revoke all on function public.purge_chapter_originals(uuid) from public;
+grant execute on function public.purge_chapter_originals(uuid) to authenticated;
